.TH SSH 1
.SH NAME
ssh \- run a command on a remote host over SSH-2
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssh
[
.B -i
.I keyfile
]
[
.B -p
.I port
]
[
.B -t
.I sha256=thumb
|
.B -n
]
[
.I user@
]\c
.I host
[
.I command ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Ssh
runs
.I command
on
.I host
over SSH-2, using this tree's own client (\c
.IR ssh (2)):
curve25519 key exchange, an
.B ssh-rsa
host key verified
.BR rsa-sha2-256 ,
and the
.B chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
cipher. It authenticates by public key, opens a session channel, execs
the command, streams its standard output and standard error to the
corresponding local file descriptors, and exits with the remote exit
status.
.PP
With no
.IR command ,
.B ssh
starts an interactive shell instead: it requests a pseudo-terminal
when
.B $TERM
is set (Plan 9's heuristic), sets the local console to raw mode (\c
.IR cons (3)'s
.BR /dev/consctl ),
and shuttles bytes between the console and the remote shell. It does no
terminal emulation of its own \(em run it inside
.IR vt (1),
which interprets the escape sequences and draws, exactly as
.IR telnet (1)
does. Window size is taken from
.B $LINES
and
.B $COLS
(default 24\(mu80).
.PP
.I Host
may be an IPv4 literal or a name;
a name is resolved through this tree's DNS-over-HTTPS resolver (\c
.IR doh (2)),
since dial-by-name is not wired up under hosted
.IR emu .
The login name is
.I user
from a
.I user@host
prefix, else the contents of
.BR /dev/user .
.SS Options
.TP
.BI -i " keyfile"
Authenticate with the PEM PKCS#1 RSA private key in
.I keyfile
(as written by
.BR "ssh-keygen -m PEM" ).
A key is required; password authentication is not offered by the
command.
.TP
.BI -p " port"
Connect to
.I port
instead of 22.
.TP
.BI -t " sha256=thumb"
Require the server's host-key thumbprint to equal
.I thumb
(the
.B sha256=
form printed on a first connection, matching OpenSSH's
.B SHA256:
fingerprint). The connection is refused on any mismatch.
.TP
.B -n
Accept an unverified host key (trust on first use), printing a warning.
Without either
.B -t
or
.BR -n ,
.B ssh
connects, prints the server's thumbprint, and refuses \(em so the user
can re-run with
.BR -t .
The host-key signature over the key exchange is always verified
regardless; these options control only whether the key's
.I identity
is trusted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Learn a host's key, then run a command:
.EX
% ssh -i $home/keyring/id_rsa me@server uname -a
ssh: host key for server is sha256=A1b2...
ssh: re-run with -t sha256=A1b2... to accept it, or -n to accept unverified
% ssh -i $home/keyring/id_rsa -t sha256=A1b2... me@server uname -a
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ssh/ssh.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ssh (2),
.IR sshtest (1),
.IR doh (2)
.SH BUGS
Public-key authentication only, from a file (no
.IR factotum (4)
key storage yet), and RSA host keys only. No port forwarding, no
.IR scp .
For a
.I command
the whole output is buffered before being written; the interactive
shell streams but does not relay a window-size change mid-session.
